  ('english-139', 'english', 'choice', '가정법과 시제', 'They spent money as if they had won the lottery.', '이 문장의 뜻은?', '[연습용 문장·표현]
They spent money as if they had won the lottery.', '복권에 당첨됐던 것처럼', array['복권에 당첨될 것처럼', '복권에 당첨돼서', '복권을 사려고'], '{}'::text[], 'as if + had p.p. = 마치 (그때 이전에) ~했던 것처럼. 실제로는 당첨되지 않았다.', '학습지3', 4, null, null, 138),
  ('english-140', 'english', 'choice', '예술과 모방', 'tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parrhasios', '여기서 that이 가리키는 것은?', 'The ancient Greeks felt that the visual artist’s goal was to copy visual experience. This approach appears in the realism of ancient Greek sculpture and pottery. We must sadly note that, due to the action of time and weather, no paintings from ancient Greek artists exist today. We can only surmise their quality based on tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parhassios, the obvious skill in ancient Greek sculpture, and in drawings that survive on ancient Greek pottery. This definition of art as copying reality has a problem, though. Jackson Pollock, a leader in the New York School of the 1950’s, intentionally did not copy existing objects in his art. While painting these works, Pollock and his fellow artists would consciously avoid making marks or passages that resembled recognizable objects. They succeeded at making artwork that did not copy anything, thus demonstrating that the ancient Greek view of art as mimesis ― simple copying ― does not sufficiently define art. 

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parrhasios', 'tale', array['quality', 'skill', 'painting'], '{}'::text[], 'that은 앞의 tales를 대신하는 지시대명사(=the tale)다. 제욱시스와 파라시오스의 일화 같은 이야기들이다.', '학습지4', 1, null, null, 139),
  ('english-141', 'english', 'choice', '예술과 모방', 'Pollock’s art copied nothing, so art as copying ______.', '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?', 'The ancient Greeks felt that the visual artist’s goal was to copy visual experience. This approach appears in the realism of ancient Greek sculpture and pottery. We must sadly note that, due to the action of time and weather, no paintings from ancient Greek artists exist today. We can only surmise their quality based on tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parhassios, the obvious skill in ancient Greek sculpture, and in drawings that survive on ancient Greek pottery. This definition of art as copying reality has a problem, though. Jackson Pollock, a leader in the New York School of the 1950’s, intentionally did not copy existing objects in his art. While painting these works, Pollock and his fellow artists would consciously avoid making marks or passages that resembled recognizable objects. They succeeded at making artwork that did not copy anything, thus demonstrating that the ancient Greek view of art as mimesis ― simple copying ― does not sufficiently define art. 

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
