  ('english-written-004', 'english', 'written', '고대 건축물', null, '‘At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system.’에서 that을 다른 관계부사로 바꿔 쓰고, 바꿀 수 있는 이유를 쓰시오.', 'Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is one of the oldest man-made structures on Earth. Experts believe it was built about 12,000 years ago. The structure consists of dozens of stone pillars arranged in rings. Many pillars are covered with carvings of animals. The tallest pillars are 5.5 meters in height and weigh more than 14,500 kilograms. At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system, and people did not use metal. Even wheels did not exist. Amazingly, though, the builders were able to cut, shape, and transport 16-ton stones. Archaeologists found Stone Age tools such as knives at the site. They think hundreds of workers carved and put the pillars in place. Archaeologists are still debating the purpose of Göbekli Tepe. Klaus Schmidt ― the archaeologist who originally excavated the site ― believed that Göbekli Tepe was a holy meeting place. According to his theory, the T-shaped pillars represent human beings. The pillars face the center of the circle and perhaps represent a religious ceremony. New evidence suggests that large feasts took place at the site. Archaeologists found thousands of small animal bones nearby, with stone containers large enough to hold more than 150 liters of liquid. They also found smaller pillars similar to Göbekli Tepe’s in areas over 200 kilometers away. It’s as though Göbekli Tepe were a cathedral and the other structures were local churches.

[연습용 문장·표현]
‘At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system.’에서 that을 다른 관계부사로 바꿔 쓰고, 바꿀 수 있는 이유를 쓰시오.', 'At the time when Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system. 선행사 the time이 시간을 나타내므로 that 자리에 관계부사 when을 쓸 수 있다.', '{}'::text[], array['that → when', '선행사 the time(시간)', '나머지 문장 그대로 유지'], '', '학습지1', 2, null, null, 1003),
  ('english-written-005', 'english', 'written', '공통 문법', null, '두 문장의 though를 비교하시오. (a) It’s as though it were a cathedral. (b) Amazingly, though, they moved the stones. 각각의 뜻과 품사를 쓰시오.', 'Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is one of the oldest man-made structures on Earth. Experts believe it was built about 12,000 years ago. The structure consists of dozens of stone pillars arranged in rings. Many pillars are covered with carvings of animals. The tallest pillars are 5.5 meters in height and weigh more than 14,500 kilograms. At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system, and people did not use metal. Even wheels did not exist. Amazingly, though, the builders were able to cut, shape, and transport 16-ton stones. Archaeologists found Stone Age tools such as knives at the site. They think hundreds of workers carved and put the pillars in place. Archaeologists are still debating the purpose of Göbekli Tepe. Klaus Schmidt ― the archaeologist who originally excavated the site ― believed that Göbekli Tepe was a holy meeting place. According to his theory, the T-shaped pillars represent human beings. The pillars face the center of the circle and perhaps represent a religious ceremony. New evidence suggests that large feasts took place at the site. Archaeologists found thousands of small animal bones nearby, with stone containers large enough to hold more than 150 liters of liquid. They also found smaller pillars similar to Göbekli Tepe’s in areas over 200 kilometers away. It’s as though Göbekli Tepe were a cathedral and the other structures were local churches.

[연습용 문장·표현]
두 문장의 though를 비교하시오. (a) It’s as though it were a cathedral. (b) Amazingly, though, they moved the stones. 각각의 뜻과 품사를 쓰시오.', '(a)의 as though는 as if와 같은 접속사로 "마치 ~인 것처럼"이며 뒤에 가정법 과거 were가 왔다. (b)의 though는 콤마 사이에 홀로 쓰인 부사로 "그러나"(=however)이다.', '{}'::text[], array['(a) as though = as if, 마치 ~인 것처럼', '(a) 가정법 과거 were', '(b) 부사, 그러나 = however'], '', '학습지1', 2, null, null, 1004),
  ('english-written-006', 'english', 'written', '공통 문법', null, 'allow와 make를 각각 사용해 ‘그 도구는 그들이 돌을 옮기게 했다.’를 과거시제로 두 문장 쓰시오.', 'For hundreds of years, Machu Picchu remained hidden in the Andes Mountains of Peru. In 1911, an American explorer named Hiram Bingham introduced the ancient city to the world after local people guided him there. Today, millions of visitors travel to see its beautiful stone buildings and breathtaking mountain views. Although many facts about Machu Picchu are known, historians still debate why the city was built and why it was later abandoned. Most experts believe that Machu Picchu was built in the 1400s during the rule of the Inca emperor Pachacuti. The city was probably home to nobles, priests, and skilled workers rather than a large population. People grew crops on stone terraces that covered the mountainside. They also collected fresh water through a system of carefully planned canals and fountains. These features allowed people to live comfortably in a place that was difficult to reach. One of the most impressive features of Machu Picchu is its stonework. The Inca builders shaped huge stones so carefully that they fit together without mortar. This method made the buildings strong enough to survive many earthquakes. Even after hundreds of years of wind, rain, and earthquakes, much of the city still stands. Machu Picchu shows that the Inca people were not only skilled builders but also talented engineers who understood both nature and architecture.

[연습용 문장·표현]
allow와 make를 각각 사용해 ‘그 도구는 그들이 돌을 옮기게 했다.’를 과거시제로 두 문장 쓰시오.', 'The tool allowed them to move the stones. / The tool made them move the stones.', '{}'::text[], array['allowed + them + to move', 'made + them + move(동사원형)', '과거시제와 the stones'], '', '학습지1', 3, null, null, 1005),
  ('english-written-007', 'english', 'written', '고대 건축물', null, '‘이 방법은 건물들을 지진을 견딜 만큼 충분히 튼튼하게 만들었다.’를 enough to를 사용해 영작하시오.', 'For hundreds of years, Machu Picchu remained hidden in the Andes Mountains of Peru. In 1911, an American explorer named Hiram Bingham introduced the ancient city to the world after local people guided him there. Today, millions of visitors travel to see its beautiful stone buildings and breathtaking mountain views. Although many facts about Machu Picchu are known, historians still debate why the city was built and why it was later abandoned. Most experts believe that Machu Picchu was built in the 1400s during the rule of the Inca emperor Pachacuti. The city was probably home to nobles, priests, and skilled workers rather than a large population. People grew crops on stone terraces that covered the mountainside. They also collected fresh water through a system of carefully planned canals and fountains. These features allowed people to live comfortably in a place that was difficult to reach. One of the most impressive features of Machu Picchu is its stonework. The Inca builders shaped huge stones so carefully that they fit together without mortar. This method made the buildings strong enough to survive many earthquakes. Even after hundreds of years of wind, rain, and earthquakes, much of the city still stands. Machu Picchu shows that the Inca people were not only skilled builders but also talented engineers who understood both nature and architecture.

[연습용 문장·표현]
‘이 방법은 건물들을 지진을 견딜 만큼 충분히 튼튼하게 만들었다.’를 enough to를 사용해 영작하시오.', 'This method made the buildings strong enough to survive earthquakes.', '{}'::text[], array['made + the buildings + 형용사 strong', 'strong enough to (enough는 형용사 뒤)', 'survive earthquakes'], '', '학습지1', 3, null, null, 1006),
  ('english-written-008', 'english', 'written', '고대 건축물', null, '‘그들은 돌을 너무 정교하게 다듬어서 돌들이 모르타르 없이 딱 맞았다.’를 so ~ that으로 영작하시오.', 'For hundreds of years, Machu Picchu remained hidden in the Andes Mountains of Peru. In 1911, an American explorer named Hiram Bingham introduced the ancient city to the world after local people guided him there. Today, millions of visitors travel to see its beautiful stone buildings and breathtaking mountain views. Although many facts about Machu Picchu are known, historians still debate why the city was built and why it was later abandoned. Most experts believe that Machu Picchu was built in the 1400s during the rule of the Inca emperor Pachacuti. The city was probably home to nobles, priests, and skilled workers rather than a large population. People grew crops on stone terraces that covered the mountainside. They also collected fresh water through a system of carefully planned canals and fountains. These features allowed people to live comfortably in a place that was difficult to reach. One of the most impressive features of Machu Picchu is its stonework. The Inca builders shaped huge stones so carefully that they fit together without mortar. This method made the buildings strong enough to survive many earthquakes. Even after hundreds of years of wind, rain, and earthquakes, much of the city still stands. Machu Picchu shows that the Inca people were not only skilled builders but also talented engineers who understood both nature and architecture.

[연습용 문장·표현]
‘그들은 돌을 너무 정교하게 다듬어서 돌들이 모르타르 없이 딱 맞았다.’를 so ~ that으로 영작하시오.', 'They shaped the stones so carefully that they fit together without mortar.', '{}'::text[], array['so carefully that', '과거시제 shaped / fit', 'together without mortar'], '', '학습지1', 3, null, null, 1007),
  ('english-written-009', 'english', 'written', '고대 건축물', null, 'excavate와 represent를 명사로 바꾸어 빈칸을 채우시오. ‘the original ___ of the site’ / ‘a symbolic ___ of humans’', 'Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is one of the oldest man-made structures on Earth. Experts believe it was built about 12,000 years ago. The structure consists of dozens of stone pillars arranged in rings. Many pillars are covered with carvings of animals. The tallest pillars are 5.5 meters in height and weigh more than 14,500 kilograms. At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system, and people did not use metal. Even wheels did not exist. Amazingly, though, the builders were able to cut, shape, and transport 16-ton stones. Archaeologists found Stone Age tools such as knives at the site. They think hundreds of workers carved and put the pillars in place. Archaeologists are still debating the purpose of Göbekli Tepe. Klaus Schmidt ― the archaeologist who originally excavated the site ― believed that Göbekli Tepe was a holy meeting place. According to his theory, the T-shaped pillars represent human beings. The pillars face the center of the circle and perhaps represent a religious ceremony. New evidence suggests that large feasts took place at the site. Archaeologists found thousands of small animal bones nearby, with stone containers large enough to hold more than 150 liters of liquid. They also found smaller pillars similar to Göbekli Tepe’s in areas over 200 kilometers away. It’s as though Göbekli Tepe were a cathedral and the other structures were local churches.

[연습용 문장·표현]
excavate와 represent를 명사로 바꾸어 빈칸을 채우시오. ‘the original ___ of the site’ / ‘a symbolic ___ of humans’', 'excavation / representation', '{}'::text[], array['excavation 철자', 'representation 철자', '두 빈칸 모두 명사형'], '', '학습지1', 2, null, null, 1008),
  ('english-written-010', 'english', 'written', 'Bartleby', null, '‘He did not work cheerfully, so I was not pleased.’를 같은 뜻의 가정법 과거완료 문장으로 바꾸시오.', 'At first, Bartleby did an extraordinary amount of writing. As if long famishing for something to copy, he eagerly copied my documents. He copied by sunlight and by candlelight. I would have been pleased with his hard work if he had worked cheerfully. But he wrote on silently, palely, mechanically. It was on the third day of his employment that the first crack appeared in this routine. I had a document that needed to be compared for accuracy. I called to Bartleby, asking him to step behind the screen to help me check a few lines. Without moving from his seat, Bartleby replied in a unusually gentle and firm voice: “I would prefer not to.” I sat in stunned silence. I thought my ears had deceived me. I repeated my request, but the response was exactly the same. “I would prefer not to.” I looked at him. He was perfectly calm. There was no sign of anger, impatience, or rudeness in his face. Had he seemed angry or rude, I would have fired him immediately. But his calmness fascinated and paralyzed me. I could not make myself argue with him. As days passed, the situation grew more bizarre. One morning, I asked him to go to the post office; he replied that he would prefer not to. I asked him to call the other clerks to help with a task; he preferred not to. Eventually, I discovered that he had stopped writing altogether. He would spend hours standing behind his screen, staring out the window at the dead brick wall. I tried to talk to him and persuade him. “Bartleby,” I said, “why will you not write? Why will you not do anything?” “I would prefer not to,” he replied, and nothing more.

My emotions toward him were a strange mixture. Sometimes I felt a deep, melancholy pity. I realized that Bartleby was alone in the world — a lonely soul in the middle of the busy, commercial desert of Wall Street. I discovered that he never ate a full dinner; he lived almost entirely on ginger nuts. Even more shockingly, I found out one Sunday morning that he was actually living in the office. He slept on the sofa, washed his face in a small basin, and spent his weekends in the total silence of the empty business district. However, pity soon turned to a sense of dread. His presence began to affect the atmosphere of the office. I found myself, and even Turkey and Nippers, unconsciously using the word ‘prefer’ in our daily speech. His passive resistance was like a quiet plague. I felt that my authority was slowly fading before a man who did nothing, said nothing, and wanted nothing. When I finally told him he must leave, he simply preferred not to. I offered him money; he let it lie on the table. I realized that as long as he remained in my office, he was my responsibility. But the pressure from my professional friends and clients became too much. They wondered why I kept such a ‘useless fixture’ in my office. Desperate to be rid of him, and unable to throw this pale, helpless man into the street, I decided on an extreme measure: I moved my entire office to a new location, leaving Bartleby behind in the empty rooms. But the story did not end there. A few days later, the new tenant of my old office came to me in a state of great excitement. Bartleby was still there. He sat on the stairs by day and slept in the entry hall by night. Everyone was disturbed. To avoid a public scandal, I went back and tried to talk to him one last time. “Bartleby," I said, “would you like to work as a store clerk?” “I would prefer not to. I am not particular,” he said. “Would you like a job traveling with someone through Europe?” “Not at all. I would prefer not to.”

[연습용 문장·표현]
‘He did not work cheerfully, so I was not pleased.’를 같은 뜻의 가정법 과거완료 문장으로 바꾸시오.', 'If he had worked cheerfully, I would have been pleased.', '{}'::text[], array['if절 had worked', '주절 would have been pleased', '부정 → 긍정으로 반대 가정'], '', '학습지3', 1, null, null, 1009),
  ('english-written-011', 'english', 'written', 'Bartleby', null, '‘If he had seemed angry, I would have fired him immediately.’에서 If를 생략한 문장을 쓰고, 실제로 일어난 일을 우리말로 쓰시오.', 'At first, Bartleby did an extraordinary amount of writing. As if long famishing for something to copy, he eagerly copied my documents. He copied by sunlight and by candlelight. I would have been pleased with his hard work if he had worked cheerfully. But he wrote on silently, palely, mechanically. It was on the third day of his employment that the first crack appeared in this routine. I had a document that needed to be compared for accuracy. I called to Bartleby, asking him to step behind the screen to help me check a few lines. Without moving from his seat, Bartleby replied in a unusually gentle and firm voice: “I would prefer not to.” I sat in stunned silence. I thought my ears had deceived me. I repeated my request, but the response was exactly the same. “I would prefer not to.” I looked at him. He was perfectly calm. There was no sign of anger, impatience, or rudeness in his face. Had he seemed angry or rude, I would have fired him immediately. But his calmness fascinated and paralyzed me. I could not make myself argue with him. As days passed, the situation grew more bizarre. One morning, I asked him to go to the post office; he replied that he would prefer not to. I asked him to call the other clerks to help with a task; he preferred not to. Eventually, I discovered that he had stopped writing altogether. He would spend hours standing behind his screen, staring out the window at the dead brick wall. I tried to talk to him and persuade him. “Bartleby,” I said, “why will you not write? Why will you not do anything?” “I would prefer not to,” he replied, and nothing more.

